WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.138
This is the technical deep dive
for Devices and Endpoints,

3
00:00:04.138 --> 00:00:07.374
one of the books of the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

4
00:00:07.374 --> 00:00:11.158
It's for the architects and
engineers who move a domain-joined

5
00:00:11.158 --> 00:00:14.125
fleet onto Intune, and its
servers onto Azure Arc.

6
00:00:14.125 --> 00:00:15.687
It comes in two parts.

7
00:00:15.687 --> 00:00:20.531
Part 1 is the endpoints: how a new device
is governed from the moment it powers on,

8
00:00:20.531 --> 00:00:24.480
the three paths an existing estate
can take to the same end state,

9
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.950
and then the Group Policy sunset and
the policy library that replaces it.

10
00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:33.264
Part 2 is servers and the run state:
bringing servers under Arc with

11
00:00:33.264 --> 00:00:37.500
a day-one baseline, then operating
and evidencing the whole fleet.

12
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:41.793
The scripts and policy payloads live
in the chapters and Appendix A,

13
00:00:41.793 --> 00:00:44.213
marked as illustrative and untested.

14
00:00:44.213 --> 00:00:45.737
I'll stick to what matters.

15
00:00:45.737 --> 00:00:49.000
First, where this session
sits in the series.

16
00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:57.271
Here's why this matters: the series
moves four populations to the cloud,

17
00:00:57.271 --> 00:01:00.700
each on its own track, and
this book carries two of them.

18
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:05.187
The Intune track is end-user devices,
and the Arc track is servers.

19
00:01:05.187 --> 00:01:10.436
A device's attributes come from device
provisioning, like the Autopilot group tag,

20
00:01:10.436 --> 00:01:13.600
never from HR, and a device
rule can't read its owner.

21
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:17.676
Devices are done when their policy
targets those attributes and

22
00:01:17.676 --> 00:01:21.450
the computer-side GPO links are
gone, one population at a time.

23
00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:26.975
Servers are done when each is Arc-enabled,
tagged, and evaluated by Azure Policy.

24
00:01:26.975 --> 00:01:30.390
And Group Policy splits: user
settings go with people,

25
00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:33.262
computer settings with
devices and servers.

26
00:01:33.262 --> 00:01:36.580
Next, why Group Policy
can't come to the cloud.

27
00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:41.717
Here's why this matters.

28
00:01:41.717 --> 00:01:44.418
For twenty years, Group
Policy did a great deal

29
00:01:44.418 --> 00:01:47.118
of governance without
anyone writing it down.

30
00:01:47.118 --> 00:01:50.019
A computer sat in an
organizational unit (OU),

31
00:01:50.019 --> 00:01:55.419
the linked GPOs applied in a fixed order,
and that one structure was the targeting,

32
00:01:55.419 --> 00:01:58.643
the precedence, the
delegation and the evidence.

33
00:01:58.643 --> 00:02:00.328
Microsoft says it plainly:

34
00:02:00.328 --> 00:02:04.618
Group Policy enforcement isn't
possible on cloud-native endpoints.

35
00:02:04.618 --> 00:02:08.917
Intune can deliver most of the
settings, but every mechanism changes.

36
00:02:08.917 --> 00:02:13.617
Targeting is flat, devices check in
about every eight hours instead of every

37
00:02:13.617 --> 00:02:18.467
ninety minutes, and when two policies
disagree, the setting may not apply at all.

38
00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:22.043
On a hybrid device, Group
Policy wins by default.

39
00:02:22.043 --> 00:02:27.471
So the program's rule is regenerate, not
copy: identify the mandatory settings,

40
00:02:27.471 --> 00:02:31.480
compare them with MDM, and
treat the gaps as blockers.

41
00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:38.342
Two more ideas frame
everything in this session.

42
00:02:38.342 --> 00:02:40.481
First, the hybrid-join trap.

43
00:02:40.481 --> 00:02:45.440
Converting a domain-joined fleet to hybrid
join is close to a tenant-wide switch:

44
00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:49.499
extend the sync scope, and every
Windows device registers itself,

45
00:02:49.499 --> 00:02:52.355
with nothing checking
ownership or approval.

46
00:02:52.355 --> 00:02:57.636
Microsoft's guidance is that Entra join
is the default for new or reset devices,

47
00:02:57.636 --> 00:03:00.642
and hybrid join is a
waypoint, not the end goal.

48
00:03:00.642 --> 00:03:04.254
Done ungoverned, the waypoint
becomes the destination.

49
00:03:04.254 --> 00:03:06.393
Second, where the truth lives.

50
00:03:06.393 --> 00:03:10.705
A laptop is described by
procurement, Autopilot, Entra ID,

51
00:03:10.705 --> 00:03:14.467
Intune and Defender records,
and a server adds Arc.

52
00:03:14.467 --> 00:03:16.662
The program decides, fact by fact,

53
00:03:16.662 --> 00:03:20.643
which one is authoritative, and
correlates on the right key.

54
00:03:20.643 --> 00:03:24.159
And one rule to remember:
delete the Entra object last,

55
00:03:24.159 --> 00:03:27.870
because that deletes the
BitLocker recovery keys.

56
00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:35.895
Module 1 is Intune-first onboarding
and the migration paths.

57
00:03:35.895 --> 00:03:39.458
Migration brings an existing
device under governance;

58
00:03:39.458 --> 00:03:43.408
Intune-first refuses to let an
ungoverned device exist at all.

59
00:03:43.408 --> 00:03:45.645
Chapter 04 covers new devices,

60
00:03:45.645 --> 00:03:50.040
and Chapter 05 the three
paths for the existing estate.

61
00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:55.752
The doctrine is simple to state.

62
00:03:55.752 --> 00:03:59.585
A new asset is governed from
the moment it first powers on:

63
00:03:59.585 --> 00:04:03.091
by Intune for endpoints, and
by Azure Arc for servers.

64
00:04:03.091 --> 00:04:06.579
There's no waiting room where it
joins Active Directory first,

65
00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:09.452
or reaches production
without a compliance check.

66
00:04:09.452 --> 00:04:12.994
Five pillars carry it, and
they're the program's doctrine,

67
00:04:12.994 --> 00:04:14.841
not Microsoft requirements.

68
00:04:14.841 --> 00:04:17.989
The ones to remember: no
ungoverned device, ever;

69
00:04:17.989 --> 00:04:23.157
procurement is the first governance
step, the way HR feeds user provisioning;

70
00:04:23.157 --> 00:04:27.679
and an onboarding failure produces
a quarantine entry and a ticket,

71
00:04:27.679 --> 00:04:30.102
never a degraded production device.

72
00:04:30.102 --> 00:04:33.108
Every asset then follows
the same five phases,

73
00:04:33.108 --> 00:04:36.115
from procure through
provision and validate.

74
00:04:36.115 --> 00:04:40.208
The organizational assignment comes from
the first source that can supply it,

75
00:04:40.208 --> 00:04:43.260
and the target is fourteen
days from purchase order.

76
00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:48.559
Here's why this matters:

77
00:04:48.559 --> 00:04:52.473
the Intune track is every end-user
device, not just Windows.

78
00:04:52.473 --> 00:04:55.466
Windows 11 and Cloud
PCs are fully managed,

79
00:04:55.466 --> 00:04:58.973
and Azure Virtual Desktop
is managed with limits.

80
00:04:58.973 --> 00:05:02.171
Apple devices come in through
Apple Business Manager,

81
00:05:02.171 --> 00:05:05.584
and Android Enterprise is
managed in all four of its modes.

82
00:05:05.584 --> 00:05:10.885
Linux desktops are limited: Ubuntu
and Red Hat, mostly for compliance.

83
00:05:10.885 --> 00:05:15.723
ChromeOS shows up as inventory, but
Google's console keeps the policy.

84
00:05:15.723 --> 00:05:20.222
Windows 10 is out of support, so it
can enroll but nothing's guaranteed.

85
00:05:20.222 --> 00:05:25.698
And servers, printers, network gear
and IoT aren't Intune platforms at all.

86
00:05:25.698 --> 00:05:30.522
Servers go to Arc, and the rest get a
tier, which we'll come to in Module 3.

87
00:05:30.522 --> 00:05:34.200
Next, Windows in a GCC tenant.

88
00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:41.400
Microsoft now ships two
ways to provision Windows.

89
00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:44.277
Autopilot device preparation is the newer,

90
00:05:44.277 --> 00:05:47.075
simpler one, with near
real-time reporting.

91
00:05:47.075 --> 00:05:49.687
Classic Autopilot is more feature-rich.

92
00:05:49.687 --> 00:05:53.157
The difference that matters
most here is registration:

93
00:05:53.157 --> 00:05:56.249
device preparation has none,
so it has no group tag.

94
00:05:56.249 --> 00:06:02.475
In a GCC tenant both are available, because
GCC uses the commercial Intune service.

95
00:06:02.475 --> 00:06:07.096
The program's default for standard hardware
is classic Autopilot, user-driven,

96
00:06:07.096 --> 00:06:11.930
with Entra join, because its registration
carries the procurement attribute and

97
00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:16.338
the purchase order from the vendor to
the device before it's ever powered on.

98
00:06:16.338 --> 00:06:19.754
Use device preparation
where its advantages matter,

99
00:06:19.754 --> 00:06:24.413
such as faster provisioning or a tenant
that might later move to GCC High.

100
00:06:24.413 --> 00:06:28.020
And never use either with
hybrid join for new devices.

101
00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:34.134
Here's how a procurement
decision reaches a

102
00:06:34.134 --> 00:06:36.958
device's configuration
without anyone touching it.

103
00:06:36.958 --> 00:06:41.244
The purchase order carries an
organizational code and the serial number.

104
00:06:41.244 --> 00:06:45.638
The supplier registers the device at
purchase, which is also what makes it

105
00:06:45.638 --> 00:06:49.958
zero-touch, after a one-time recorded
approval by a Global Administrator.

106
00:06:49.958 --> 00:06:54.462
That group tag shows up on the Entra
device, a dynamic group matches on it,

107
00:06:54.462 --> 00:06:58.508
and the profile, compliance and
configuration follow the group.

108
00:06:58.508 --> 00:07:02.810
The tag holds a node identifier,
never a free-text department name,

109
00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:07.908
so a reorganization changes a mapping
table, not thousands of registrations.

110
00:07:07.908 --> 00:07:11.067
Enrollment restrictions
are the enforcement point:

111
00:07:11.067 --> 00:07:14.872
block personal Windows enrollment,
and an unregistered device

112
00:07:14.872 --> 00:07:18.820
simply fails to enroll, which is
the quarantine pillar in practice.

113
00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:22.590
The slide covers naming and
the Enrollment Status Page.

114
00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:31.478
A new device should arrive at a known baseline
before anyone uses it, in two halves.

115
00:07:31.478 --> 00:07:33.428
Compliance decides access.

116
00:07:33.428 --> 00:07:38.266
The first step is a tenant-wide setting:
mark devices with no compliance policy

117
00:07:38.266 --> 00:07:43.328
as not compliant, so an unassigned device
can't pass Conditional Access by default.

118
00:07:43.328 --> 00:07:46.813
The Windows policy then
requires the usual protections,

119
00:07:46.813 --> 00:07:49.853
like BitLocker, Secure
Boot and current Defender.

120
00:07:49.853 --> 00:07:54.902
Configuration sets the device up, starting
from Microsoft's security baselines.

121
00:07:54.902 --> 00:07:57.579
Because these devices
never had Group Policy,

122
00:07:57.579 --> 00:08:00.678
there's nothing to conflict
with, and that's the payoff.

123
00:08:00.678 --> 00:08:04.004
Four exception paths cover what
can't follow the main path,

124
00:08:04.004 --> 00:08:07.816
from personal devices to Windows
machines that can't enroll at all.

125
00:08:07.816 --> 00:08:13.128
Each needs written justification, a
compensating control and an audit entry,

126
00:08:13.128 --> 00:08:15.810
and the last one is isolated first.

127
00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:22.960
Chapter 05 is how the
existing estate gets there.

128
00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:28.150
The sources described three separate kits
that repeated most of the same steps,

129
00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:33.025
because the destination is the same:
servers in Arc, workstations in Intune,

130
00:08:33.025 --> 00:08:37.585
NTLM restricted after it's measured,
and Conditional Access enforced.

131
00:08:37.585 --> 00:08:40.085
What differs is who holds
the decision record.

132
00:08:40.085 --> 00:08:45.685
The rule all three obey: the control
plane governs, the data plane executes.

133
00:08:45.685 --> 00:08:49.491
Every path runs four phases
in order, observe, onboard,

134
00:08:49.491 --> 00:08:54.335
harden and watch, and nothing is
restricted until it's been measured.

135
00:08:54.335 --> 00:08:57.435
The manual path suits
small estates and pilots.

136
00:08:57.435 --> 00:09:01.310
The governed path puts an approval
reference on every change.

137
00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:04.216
The retrofit adopts a
hand-modernized estate,

138
00:09:04.216 --> 00:09:07.198
at the risk of blessing
whatever drift is there.

139
00:09:07.198 --> 00:09:12.270
And the federal ceiling is human
approval; autonomous action is refused.

140
00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:19.832
Three places in the shared
runbook cause most of the outages.

141
00:09:19.832 --> 00:09:21.107
Precedence first.

142
00:09:21.107 --> 00:09:26.633
Existing devices usually enroll in Intune
through Group Policy, with no reimage.

143
00:09:26.633 --> 00:09:31.277
But while the GPOs are still linked,
overlaps are dangerous: Group Policy

144
00:09:31.277 --> 00:09:35.845
usually wins, and for some settings
it's a race with no guaranteed winner.

145
00:09:35.845 --> 00:09:40.045
So document every overlap before
Intune enforces anything.

146
00:09:40.045 --> 00:09:42.007
NTLM second.

147
00:09:42.007 --> 00:09:47.684
A common mistake is treating the strictest
LAN Manager level as blocking NTLM;

148
00:09:47.684 --> 00:09:51.963
it doesn't, because domain
controllers still accept NTLMv2.

149
00:09:51.963 --> 00:09:55.630
Blocking takes the Restrict
NTLM policies, staged,

150
00:09:55.630 --> 00:10:00.171
and only after a fourteen to thirty
day audit and owner sign-off.

151
00:10:00.171 --> 00:10:01.632
And SPNs third.

152
00:10:01.632 --> 00:10:08.007
A missing SPN is the silent case: Kerberos
isn't tried and NTLM quietly works,

153
00:10:08.007 --> 00:10:12.070
until the NTLM block
turns it into an outage.

154
00:10:15.820 --> 00:10:20.658
Module 2 is the GPO sunset, the
program for the existing fleet.

155
00:10:20.658 --> 00:10:24.519
It replaces Group Policy on
endpoints already deployed,

156
00:10:24.519 --> 00:10:29.283
and it's measured by GPOs retired with
evidence, not by settings copied.

157
00:10:29.283 --> 00:10:35.150
Chapters 07 and 08 run it, and
Appendix A is the crosswalk.

158
00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:44.070
The program keeps three efforts apart:
Intune-first for new devices, this sunset

159
00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:49.475
for the existing fleet, and regenerate-not-copy
as the way replacements are written.

160
00:10:49.475 --> 00:10:54.550
Microsoft's own advice is that assessing
every setting is likely impractical,

161
00:10:54.550 --> 00:10:58.375
so the program runs seven steps,
each with an exit condition.

162
00:10:58.375 --> 00:11:02.431
Step one: nothing is translated or
retired until it's inventoried,

163
00:11:02.431 --> 00:11:07.238
including its targeting, because that's
the part that doesn't survive the move.

164
00:11:07.238 --> 00:11:10.504
Group Policy analytics then
shows, setting by setting,

165
00:11:10.504 --> 00:11:14.425
what's ready to migrate, what isn't
supported and what's deprecated.

166
00:11:14.425 --> 00:11:17.213
Plan around its limits,
which are on the slide.

167
00:11:17.213 --> 00:11:21.374
Its migrate step is, in
Microsoft's words, best effort.

168
00:11:21.374 --> 00:11:24.697
So the program treats the
readiness report as proof that

169
00:11:24.697 --> 00:11:28.090
an equivalent exists, never
that a setting is needed.

170
00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:38.115
Step two gives every setting one tag:
required, obsolete, high-risk or redundant.

171
00:11:38.115 --> 00:11:40.128
Only required settings move on.

172
00:11:40.128 --> 00:11:44.703
Each one then faces four questions
in order, and the first yes decides.

173
00:11:44.703 --> 00:11:46.840
Is it still needed in the target model?

174
00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:51.340
Group Policy accumulated settings that
existed only because it could set them.

175
00:11:51.340 --> 00:11:53.603
Does Intune have a native equivalent?

176
00:11:53.603 --> 00:11:54.514
Most do.

177
00:11:54.514 --> 00:11:56.615
Does a cloud pattern remove the need?

178
00:11:56.615 --> 00:12:01.011
Folder redirection, for example,
becomes OneDrive Known Folder Move,

179
00:12:01.011 --> 00:12:03.865
and mapped printers
become Universal Print.

180
00:12:03.865 --> 00:12:05.903
And can a script enforce and prove it?

181
00:12:05.903 --> 00:12:09.424
A setting that fails all four
becomes a dated exception,

182
00:12:09.424 --> 00:12:12.564
and its GPO stays linked
for that population only.

183
00:12:12.564 --> 00:12:16.206
The answers send settings to
four landing zones in Intune,

184
00:12:16.206 --> 00:12:18.460
and the slide has their limits.

185
00:12:22.210 --> 00:12:25.691
While a population has both
GPOs and Intune policies,

186
00:12:25.691 --> 00:12:27.985
three conflict systems are in play.

187
00:12:27.985 --> 00:12:32.673
Inside Intune, configuration policies
have no precedence over each other.

188
00:12:32.673 --> 00:12:36.007
A conflict is reported and
the setting might not apply,

189
00:12:36.007 --> 00:12:38.897
so each setting gets
exactly one owning policy.

190
00:12:38.897 --> 00:12:43.589
The chapter also corrects earlier program
material: there's no last-write-wins,

191
00:12:43.589 --> 00:12:48.136
and endpoint security doesn't
automatically beat configuration profiles.

192
00:12:48.136 --> 00:12:53.407
Between Group Policy and MDM, Group Policy
keeps winning unless you change that,

193
00:12:53.407 --> 00:12:56.522
and outside the core policy
settings it's a race.

194
00:12:56.522 --> 00:12:59.227
Between Configuration Manager and Intune,

195
00:12:59.227 --> 00:13:03.434
the co-management slider decides,
with a couple of traps on the slide.

196
00:13:03.434 --> 00:13:08.557
Disabling a GPO link is the moment
production behavior changes, so it's gated:

197
00:13:08.557 --> 00:13:11.483
freeze, back up, prove
the replacement works,

198
00:13:11.483 --> 00:13:15.260
then disable the link with
one reversible command.

199
00:13:19.010 --> 00:13:24.017
Chapter 08 breaks the seven phases into
working tasks, with durations from the

200
00:13:24.017 --> 00:13:29.410
program's reference plan for a large fleet
that already runs Configuration Manager.

201
00:13:29.410 --> 00:13:34.138
First comes the inventory and classification,
then co-management for a pilot,

202
00:13:34.138 --> 00:13:39.235
then building every policy unassigned, with
the riskier protections in audit mode.

203
00:13:39.235 --> 00:13:43.490
The pilot is about five percent of
the fleet, representative across

204
00:13:43.490 --> 00:13:47.973
tiers and regions, and the GPOs stay
linked so conflicts can be measured.

205
00:13:47.973 --> 00:13:53.383
Then four waves of three weeks each, and
finally the links and GPOs are disabled,

206
00:13:53.383 --> 00:13:56.047
kept ninety days, backed up and deleted.

207
00:13:56.047 --> 00:14:01.385
On these estimates, the program reaches the
last phase in roughly thirty-four weeks.

208
00:14:01.385 --> 00:14:04.892
One reminder: rolling back
a pilot means reassigning a

209
00:14:04.892 --> 00:14:09.060
policy with the old value, not
just removing the assignment.

210
00:14:12.810 --> 00:14:17.622
The policy library has five types, and
every policy is assigned to dynamic

211
00:14:17.622 --> 00:14:22.197
device groups built from attributes,
which replaces OU-based scoping.

212
00:14:22.197 --> 00:14:26.740
Those attributes are the device's own
extension attributes, written through

213
00:14:26.740 --> 00:14:30.786
Microsoft Graph, with one numbering
and one fixed spelling per value.

214
00:14:30.786 --> 00:14:35.635
The correction that matters: assignment
filters can't read extension attributes.

215
00:14:35.635 --> 00:14:38.621
Attribute targeting
belongs in the group rule;

216
00:14:38.621 --> 00:14:42.297
filters work on Intune properties
like join type or model.

217
00:14:42.297 --> 00:14:45.711
Three Microsoft facts shape
every compliance policy.

218
00:14:45.711 --> 00:14:49.235
Noncompliance actions are
scheduled in days, not hours.

219
00:14:49.235 --> 00:14:50.617
There's no block action;

220
00:14:50.617 --> 00:14:54.835
blocking is what Conditional Access
does with a noncompliant device.

221
00:14:54.835 --> 00:14:58.040
And encryption and boot
checks are measured at boot,

222
00:14:58.040 --> 00:15:00.723
so a newly encrypted
device needs a restart.

223
00:15:00.723 --> 00:15:05.520
The slide has the compliance set,
including a strict Tier 0 policy.

224
00:15:09.270 --> 00:15:12.786
Endpoint security policies
aren't a higher-priority source;

225
00:15:12.786 --> 00:15:16.020
they have the same standing
as configuration profiles.

226
00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:20.839
In the program's library, though, they
own every Defender, BitLocker, firewall,

227
00:15:20.839 --> 00:15:23.210
account protection and LAPS setting,

228
00:15:23.210 --> 00:15:27.570
and the settings catalog profiles
that duplicated them are retired.

229
00:15:27.570 --> 00:15:29.632
The slide walks through each one.

230
00:15:29.632 --> 00:15:30.858
A few highlights.

231
00:15:30.858 --> 00:15:35.393
Sending every file sample to Microsoft
can upload organizational data,

232
00:15:35.393 --> 00:15:37.207
so that's the data owner's call.

233
00:15:37.207 --> 00:15:41.423
Silent encryption and a TPM PIN
can't apply to the same device,

234
00:15:41.423 --> 00:15:44.120
so Tier 0 gets its own BitLocker policy.

235
00:15:44.120 --> 00:15:48.657
On an Entra-joined device, the
firewall's domain profile doesn't apply.

236
00:15:48.657 --> 00:15:53.583
And attack surface reduction rules run in
audit for thirty days before blocking.

237
00:15:53.583 --> 00:15:55.987
Appendix A flags the crosswalk traps,

238
00:15:55.987 --> 00:16:00.300
such as a logon message breaking
Autopilot pre-provisioning.

239
00:16:04.050 --> 00:16:06.308
Module 3 is servers under Azure Arc.

240
00:16:06.308 --> 00:16:08.799
Intune doesn't support Windows Server,

241
00:16:08.799 --> 00:16:12.225
so Arc takes over the job
Group Policy did for servers.

242
00:16:12.225 --> 00:16:15.133
Arc gives a server an
address in the control plane;

243
00:16:15.133 --> 00:16:19.110
what governs it is everything
you then attach to that address.

244
00:16:22.860 --> 00:16:26.148
Arc is the management plane,
not the identity plane.

245
00:16:26.148 --> 00:16:29.979
Onboarding doesn't change how
a server authenticates users or

246
00:16:29.979 --> 00:16:33.884
whether it's domain-joined, so keep
the two separate in every plan.

247
00:16:33.884 --> 00:16:38.695
What changes: the server becomes an
Azure resource, with role-based access,

248
00:16:38.695 --> 00:16:43.110
tags and Azure Policy, and extensions
that run with full privileges.

249
00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:47.422
What doesn't: its domain membership,
its workload and where it runs.

250
00:16:47.422 --> 00:16:49.398
Arc isn't a migration to Azure.

251
00:16:49.398 --> 00:16:51.497
Two properties shape operations.

252
00:16:51.497 --> 00:16:53.223
Azure is the source of truth,

253
00:16:53.223 --> 00:16:56.673
so every change is checked
before it reaches the server.

254
00:16:56.673 --> 00:16:58.922
And connection state is evidence.

255
00:16:58.922 --> 00:17:03.407
A server disconnected for fourteen
days loses its baseline assignments,

256
00:17:03.407 --> 00:17:05.760
and after forty-five days it can expire.

257
00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:09.175
Plan cost by service, too;
with the right licensing,

258
00:17:09.175 --> 00:17:14.280
several management services come at
no extra cost, once you attest it.

259
00:17:18.030 --> 00:17:21.887
Here's why this matters: the Arc
track is servers wherever they run,

260
00:17:21.887 --> 00:17:24.530
and the inventory needs
everything else too.

261
00:17:24.530 --> 00:17:30.388
Arc covers Windows Server 2016 through
2025, the supported Linux distributions,

262
00:17:30.388 --> 00:17:35.109
physical servers and any hypervisor,
VMware VMs through vCenter,

263
00:17:35.109 --> 00:17:40.617
and AWS and Google Cloud instances, with
Google's connector still in preview.

264
00:17:40.617 --> 00:17:42.266
Azure VMs don't need Arc.

265
00:17:42.266 --> 00:17:46.692
AIX, Solaris, the mainframe and
network devices aren't covered.

266
00:17:46.692 --> 00:17:52.496
So every device lands in one of four tiers:
Intune or Arc; its own tool, with the

267
00:17:52.496 --> 00:17:54.742
CMDB holding its attributes;

268
00:17:54.742 --> 00:17:59.142
discovered and monitored only;
or segmented and retired.

269
00:17:59.142 --> 00:18:03.005
A device in no tier is a finding
against the inventory control.

270
00:18:03.005 --> 00:18:05.540
Next, getting ready to onboard.

271
00:18:09.290 --> 00:18:12.428
Before the first server
connects, settle four things.

272
00:18:12.428 --> 00:18:13.553
Roles first.

273
00:18:13.553 --> 00:18:15.456
Use the narrowest built-in roles,

274
00:18:15.456 --> 00:18:19.827
and remember that extensions run
arbitrary scripts with full privileges.

275
00:18:19.827 --> 00:18:24.192
Microsoft says to treat any Contributor
on an Arc resource as an indirect

276
00:18:24.192 --> 00:18:27.625
administrator of the server,
so scope those roles tightly

277
00:18:27.625 --> 00:18:30.916
and hold them through
Privileged Identity Management.

278
00:18:30.916 --> 00:18:35.905
Network: the agent only talks outbound
over 443, with no inbound rule,

279
00:18:35.905 --> 00:18:40.628
and a private link scope keeps
that traffic off public networks.

280
00:18:40.628 --> 00:18:44.627
Run the agent's connectivity check
from a representative server first.

281
00:18:44.627 --> 00:18:47.708
Tags: settle one taxonomy
at connection time,

282
00:18:47.708 --> 00:18:51.953
because tags drive policy scope,
patching and cost reporting.

283
00:18:51.953 --> 00:18:55.351
And images: install the
agent in your golden image,

284
00:18:55.351 --> 00:19:00.180
but never connect the image, or
every clone shares one resource ID.

285
00:19:03.930 --> 00:19:05.718
Onboarding runs in waves.

286
00:19:05.718 --> 00:19:10.067
Each wave creates its credential,
connects a set of servers, attaches the

287
00:19:10.067 --> 00:19:14.779
baseline on the same change, verifies,
and then removes what onboarding needed.

288
00:19:14.779 --> 00:19:17.130
Microsoft supports many methods.

289
00:19:17.130 --> 00:19:21.371
The program recommends a dedicated
service principal that signs in with a

290
00:19:21.371 --> 00:19:25.680
certificate, deployed through the
automation the server team already runs.

291
00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:29.217
That credential is needed only
while the connect command runs,

292
00:19:29.217 --> 00:19:31.530
so keep its life short
and its scope small.

293
00:19:31.530 --> 00:19:33.824
Someone holding it could connect servers

294
00:19:33.824 --> 00:19:36.642
outside your organization
into your subscription.

295
00:19:36.642 --> 00:19:40.042
Protect it in deployment,
too: a secret in a hand-built

296
00:19:40.042 --> 00:19:44.231
startup script is readable by
every domain user through SYSVOL.

297
00:19:44.231 --> 00:19:48.485
And for sensitive servers, set
local controls Azure can't override,

298
00:19:48.485 --> 00:19:50.690
like an extension allowlist.

299
00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:57.672
A connected server is
addressable, not yet governed.

300
00:19:57.672 --> 00:20:00.237
Three things get attached
on the same change.

301
00:20:00.237 --> 00:20:03.525
Configuration, through
Azure machine configuration,

302
00:20:03.525 --> 00:20:08.644
which audits or sets operating system
settings and reports compliance per machine,

303
00:20:08.644 --> 00:20:11.204
scoped by Azure resource
rather than by OU.

304
00:20:11.204 --> 00:20:15.884
Keep the customized baseline in the
governance repository as policy-as-code,

305
00:20:15.884 --> 00:20:19.614
and treat noncompliance as a
finding, not a failed deployment.

306
00:20:19.614 --> 00:20:23.339
Patching, through Azure Update
Manager, which assesses daily,

307
00:20:23.339 --> 00:20:26.846
patches in maintenance windows
and groups servers by tag.

308
00:20:26.846 --> 00:20:29.488
And protection, through
Defender for Servers.

309
00:20:29.488 --> 00:20:32.908
The program's FedRAMP
Moderate scope uses Plan 2,

310
00:20:32.908 --> 00:20:35.873
plus Defender for SQL on SQL hosts.

311
00:20:35.873 --> 00:20:39.839
Onboard Defender through Arc,
rather than installing it directly,

312
00:20:39.839 --> 00:20:42.350
which gives you only Plan 1 function.

313
00:20:46.100 --> 00:20:49.621
Arc is also how many organizations
keep end-of-support servers

314
00:20:49.621 --> 00:20:52.938
patched while they migrate, and
the dates are the point here.

315
00:20:52.938 --> 00:20:55.235
As of October twentieth, 2026,

316
00:20:55.235 --> 00:21:01.648
Windows Server 2012 and 2012 R2 receive
no further security updates through any

317
00:21:01.648 --> 00:21:05.189
channel, so any still
running is a red finding:

318
00:21:05.189 --> 00:21:09.113
migrate, upgrade, or isolate
it and record the risk.

319
00:21:09.113 --> 00:21:14.141
Windows Server 2016's end of
support is January twelfth, 2027,

320
00:21:14.141 --> 00:21:19.874
so any host that remains is an exception:
enroll it in ESUs before then.

321
00:21:19.874 --> 00:21:23.599
Arc servers can also use
Entra ID for Remote Desktop,

322
00:21:23.599 --> 00:21:28.277
but only on Server 2025 or Windows
11 24H2, and Conditional Access

323
00:21:28.277 --> 00:21:33.387
isn't supported there; the control is
role-based access held through PIM.

324
00:21:33.387 --> 00:21:39.100
And rollback has an order: remove
extensions, disconnect, then uninstall.

325
00:21:42.850 --> 00:21:46.238
Module 4 is operating
and evidencing the fleet.

326
00:21:46.238 --> 00:21:51.335
Once the GPOs are gone, a device's state
has to be proved from three records:

327
00:21:51.335 --> 00:21:56.601
the policy library, the status Intune
and Defender report, and the audit log.

328
00:21:56.601 --> 00:21:59.250
Operating means keeping them in agreement.

329
00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:08.102
Compliance policies decide whether a device
is compliant; Conditional Access decides

330
00:22:08.102 --> 00:22:12.575
what a noncompliant device can reach,
and Access and Privilege owns that part.

331
00:22:12.575 --> 00:22:15.338
This book's job is making
the signal accurate.

332
00:22:15.338 --> 00:22:18.412
Two tenant settings
sit above every policy.

333
00:22:18.412 --> 00:22:23.688
Devices with no compliance policy default
to compliant, and the program flips that,

334
00:22:23.688 --> 00:22:27.663
so a device that falls outside
every group can't pass by default.

335
00:22:27.663 --> 00:22:31.559
And compliance status stays
valid for thirty days by default;

336
00:22:31.559 --> 00:22:35.379
the program uses fourteen, short
enough that a device left in

337
00:22:35.379 --> 00:22:39.199
a drawer loses access, long enough
not to catch staff on leave.

338
00:22:39.199 --> 00:22:40.972
Read the dashboard carefully,

339
00:22:40.972 --> 00:22:44.800
because some statuses hide devices
from the noncompliant count.

340
00:22:44.800 --> 00:22:49.306
And on Tier 0, a Defender detection
becomes a compliance failure,

341
00:22:49.306 --> 00:22:51.200
and so a loss of access.

342
00:22:54.950 --> 00:22:58.660
Intune manages Windows updates
through four policy types,

343
00:22:58.660 --> 00:23:01.612
and the rings stage each
month's quality update.

344
00:23:01.612 --> 00:23:05.312
The ring names follow Windows
Autopatch, Test, First,

345
00:23:05.312 --> 00:23:09.613
Fast and Broad, from the engineering
lab out to everyone else.

346
00:23:09.613 --> 00:23:11.675
The values are the program's design.

347
00:23:11.675 --> 00:23:16.795
With them, a quality update reaches every
online device within about sixteen days

348
00:23:16.795 --> 00:23:21.538
of release, and an update that breaks an
early ring can be paused before Broad.

349
00:23:21.538 --> 00:23:26.289
Autopatch automates all of
this, and it's included for

350
00:23:26.289 --> 00:23:31.150
GCC customers with G3 or G5,
though not in GCC High or DoD.

351
00:23:31.150 --> 00:23:35.175
It maintains its own rings, so
don't assign yours to its devices.

352
00:23:35.175 --> 00:23:42.059
And version currency: 23H2 Enterprise
stops receiving updates on November tenth,

353
00:23:42.059 --> 00:23:45.770
2026, so feature updates target 25H2 now.

354
00:23:49.520 --> 00:23:54.277
Intune, Entra ID and Defender each
keep their own device inventory,

355
00:23:54.277 --> 00:23:57.045
and they disagree in predictable ways.

356
00:23:57.045 --> 00:24:00.757
The program reconciles them
weekly on the Entra device ID.

357
00:24:00.757 --> 00:24:05.150
Every enrolled device needs an Entra
object with its attributes set and

358
00:24:05.150 --> 00:24:09.470
a healthy Defender sensor, and a device
missing from any one is a finding.

359
00:24:09.470 --> 00:24:13.145
That same comparison is the
evidence for a complete inventory.

360
00:24:13.145 --> 00:24:18.346
Treat stale records with care: cleanup
rules hide devices but don't delete them,

361
00:24:18.346 --> 00:24:23.307
and Entra's sign-in date isn't updated
every time, so disable before deleting.

362
00:24:23.307 --> 00:24:25.254
Retirement follows a fixed order,

363
00:24:25.254 --> 00:24:28.382
because the order decides
what can still be recovered.

364
00:24:28.382 --> 00:24:33.800
Export the BitLocker keys and LAPS password
first, then wipe, deregister and delete,

365
00:24:33.800 --> 00:24:39.110
and delete the Entra object last, because
that permanently removes its keys.

366
00:24:42.860 --> 00:24:47.025
Drift is a gap between the policy
library and what a device reports,

367
00:24:47.025 --> 00:24:52.022
one the policy should have prevented, like
BitLocker off or an OS below the floor.

368
00:24:52.022 --> 00:24:56.666
The program finds it with the same loop
as the governed path: a daily export,

369
00:24:56.666 --> 00:25:00.701
a comparison with the library,
classification, and remediation,

370
00:25:00.701 --> 00:25:03.898
with policy changes going
through change control.

371
00:25:03.898 --> 00:25:05.209
The catch is timing.

372
00:25:05.209 --> 00:25:09.862
Intune's audit logs arrive in Log
Analytics right away, but compliance and

373
00:25:09.862 --> 00:25:14.972
inventory data are exported once a day and
can take up to forty-eight hours to land.

374
00:25:14.972 --> 00:25:19.111
So those tables are for trends and
evidence, not minute-level alerts.

375
00:25:19.111 --> 00:25:22.607
That's why fleet-wide alerts
come from the daily export,

376
00:25:22.607 --> 00:25:26.260
but Tier 0 noncompliance is
polled from Graph every hour.

377
00:25:26.260 --> 00:25:29.570
Otherwise the alert would
arrive two days late.

378
00:25:33.320 --> 00:25:35.195
Last, what the assessor reads.

379
00:25:35.195 --> 00:25:38.471
The evidence is the records
the services already keep,

380
00:25:38.471 --> 00:25:42.401
exported on a schedule and retained,
mapped to the configuration

381
00:25:42.401 --> 00:25:48.370
management and flaw remediation
controls in NIST SP 800-53.

382
00:25:48.370 --> 00:25:52.682
Baseline configuration is the policy
library under version control.

383
00:25:52.682 --> 00:25:55.545
Inventory is the weekly reconciliation.

384
00:25:55.545 --> 00:25:57.819
Flaw remediation is the ring table,

385
00:25:57.819 --> 00:26:01.270
whose deadlines set the
organization's patch period.

386
00:26:01.270 --> 00:26:03.408
Two GCC facts matter.

387
00:26:03.408 --> 00:26:06.139
Intune in GCC uses the commercial service,

388
00:26:06.139 --> 00:26:11.369
and Windows Update for Business reports
don't meet GCC compliance requirements,

389
00:26:11.369 --> 00:26:15.194
so patch evidence comes from
Intune and Autopatch reports.

390
00:26:15.194 --> 00:26:20.253
So, from Monday: inventory and triage
every GPO, set the compliance defaults,

391
00:26:20.253 --> 00:26:24.332
carry procurement into the group
tag, onboard servers with a

392
00:26:24.332 --> 00:26:29.145
short-lived certificate principal,
and reconcile inventories weekly.

393
00:26:29.145 --> 00:26:29.960
Thank you.
